<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Lahsa Eyalet</h1><p class="st-meta">1560 CE – 1670 CE (approx.) · capital Hofuf</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Ottoman province of eastern Arabia, based at Hofuf, which guarded the Gulf flank of the empire for a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> extended its reach down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Gulf</a> coast and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> around 1550, partly to counter the Portuguese. The province of Lahsa was organised around 1560 with its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hofuf">Hofuf</a> and included the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governors held the oasis towns with small garrisons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> and local levies, collecting taxes from the large date-growing oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> and customs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a>. The province served as a base against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-bahrain">Portuguese Bahrain</a>, though an Ottoman expedition to take the island in 1559 failed. Control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> hinterland was always weak, and governors were often isolated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1670 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barrak-ibn-ghurayr">Barrak ibn Ghurayr</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banu-khalid-emirate">Banu Khalid</a> expelled the Ottoman garrison and founded the Banu Khalid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> only in 1871.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lahsa-eyalet.jpg" alt="Lahsa Eyalet" width="320" height="358"><figcaption>Image: Karl Spruner von Merz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lahsa_Eyalet_Map_1560-1670.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1560 CE – 1670 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hofuf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ottoman Turkish, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eyalet of al-Hasa, Ottoman al-Hasa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1615&amp;focus=lahsa-eyalet" aria-label="Lahsa Eyalet on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5998 1203 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lahsa Eyalet lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6318 1459L5709 970M6318 1459L6314 1501"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M6314 1501h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6318 1459h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6318 1459h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1615&amp;focus=lahsa-eyalet">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
